-
-export const theTypecheckLeavesARouterAppsRootRouteToTheAppsOwnCompile = {
-  id: "01a06935-0be3-7ef6-b1a0-ab3570179fe5",
-  pageTypeSlug: "finding",
-  slug: "the-typecheck-leaves-a-router-apps-root-route-to-the-apps-own-compile",
-  domainSlug: "domain/akasha-check",
-  claim:
-    "Under the `Alan Approves Checks` directive, with approvals released for the migration, I narrowed what the `typecheck` code-check judges. `routingIn` now yields a router app's `root.tsx` beside its `routes/` folder, so `rootsOf` drops the root route, and five `root.tsx` files stopped refusing `TS2307 Cannot find module './+types/root'`. The alternative was not available rather than merely costly: `rootDirs` in the shared `SETTINGS` resolves none of them. Two further refusals of the same message, in `alan/web/.server`, were a different class, and I fixed those rather than excluding them.",
-  evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-03 in this repository, react-router 7.15.1.\n\nWHAT I CHANGED. `typecheck.code-check.code.ts:75-84`: `routingIn` returned one folder per router app, `<app>/routes/`; it now returns that and `<app>/root.tsx`, spelled as `rootRoute.fileName` from `@akasha/code-system/router-app/root-route` rather than as a second literal. `routed` and `rootsOf` are untouched, and `routed` matches an exact file path through the same `startsWith` it already used for a folder. `code-system/package.json` gained the export the import needs. The check's own page gained `A router app's root route is not judged.`, `A router app's generated route types stand outside the repository.`, and its `gap` now reads `A router app's routes and its root route are judged by the app's own compile.` Landed as e7a1120a43.\n\nWHY NOT `rootDirs`. Probed by building the program three ways over the same seven files. `SETTINGS` as they stand: 7 refusals. `SETTINGS` plus `rootDirs: [\".\", \"./.react-router/types\"]` at the repository root: the same 7, none fixed, because `rootDirs` maps a path suffix and `akasha/alan/web/root.tsx` then looks under `.react-router/types/akasha/alan/web/+types/`. Only a ten-entry list naming every app and its own generated tree resolved the five root routes, which is the list in the check that `Derived Reach` refuses. So the shared setting does not trade breadth for a fix; it buys nothing.\n\nWHY THE EXCLUSION COSTS NO COVERAGE. `akasha/alan/web/.gitignore:5` ignores `.react-router`, so the generated types are absent from the repository and from the index; this program can never resolve them. The app's own compile can: parsing `akasha/alan/web/tsconfig.json` through the TypeScript API gives a 358-file program holding `root.tsx`, whose `rootDirs` are the app folder and its `.react-router/types`, and `root.tsx` draws 0 TS2307 there. The `gap` invariant is therefore true of the root route as it was of the routes folder.\n\nWHERE I DISAGREED. The two `.server` modules were not the same class and excluding them would have buried a live defect. `app-layout-loading` imported `./+types/_app-layout` and `page-detail-loading` imported `./+types/page-detail`, but typegen writes those at `.react-router/types/routes/+types/`, which is the mapping for a file in `routes/`. Both modules sit two folders away, so no `rootDirs` reaches them: the ten-entry probe left both refusing. The app's own compile reaches both through the `export { loader } from` in `routes/_app-layout.tsx` and reported the same two TS2307. They were broken in every program that looked. Both now take `LoaderFunctionArgs` from react-router, which is what the generated type narrows, and `page-detail-loading` already guarded its params against `undefined`. Landed as 827e5d483f; both are clean in this check and in the app's own compile, and both are still judged.\n\nPROOF. Before: `akasha typecheck` over the seven named 7 refusals. After: `foundIn` over the same seven as one change reports 0. Seeded, the same call reports the seeded TS2322 in each of the two `.server` modules, so the instrument still looks at what it still judges. Seeding one ordinary module in each of the five apps draws a fault from all five, so the exclusion took the root route alone and not the app. The five root routes now answer `was not judged` with exit 3 rather than a clean pass, so the narrowing is visible at the command rather than silent.\n\nFOR ALAN. This narrows a judging check, which `Zero At Turning On` warns against. The reading that makes it right is that the root route is the same class as the routes folder already excluded, and the measurement above is the whole of my case. The wider prize I did not take: a `rootDirs` derived per program from the router apps the index names, rather than stated in `SETTINGS`, would let this check judge the 60-odd route files it excludes today. That is turning reach on, so it wants its own zero first.",
-} as const satisfies Finding
